  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Limited threat for severe weather into early this \nevening.\n\nInitial round of showers and embedded thunderstorms working through \nthe North Country early this afternoon. This area of convection \nfocused on the nose of a low level jet and along a nearly stationary \nboundary draped along the northern portions of New York State. This \nactivity will likely lift northward through this afternoon, with \nwestern and north central New York remaining within the warm sector. \nModest heating and lapse rates will limit destabilization this \nafternoon. However, the presence of dewpoints in the low-mid 60s and \nlow-level hodograph curvatures will favor the potential for some \norganized convection to develop later this afternoon. There is a \nLIMITED threat for localized damaging wind gusts. SPC continues to \npromote a Marginal Risk for Severe Thunderstorms this afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Colder weather by the end of the week with some \npotential for snow. \n\nA deepening upper level trough will progress eastward into the Great \nLakes region Thursday night and Friday. The associated surface low \nwill strengthen across the Great Lakes, supporting a sharp cold \nfront to sweep through on Friday. Confidence remains low at this \ntime as model discrepancies with the timing, strength and placement \nof this system persists. Regardless, the atmospheric pattern will \nlikely bring the potential for strong winds Friday into Saturday. \nAdditionally, a much colder airmass will surge into the area in the \nwake of the frontal passage with pre-frontal synoptic rain showers \neventually giving way to lake effect rain and snow showers.",
